WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.770
Philip Payton Jr. was a turn of the century
real estate agent

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.840
famous for outwitting his adversaries,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.700
and posting his red initials to every building
under his control.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.080
He enabled generations of black New Yorkers
to inhabit prime areas of Manhattan,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.860
with modern homes, nearby parks, and free
from racial violence.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.980
Payton was a crusading capitalist,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.920
who believed that the free-market was the
key to combating racial segregation.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.180
His approach was "to make the color line costly."

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.040
In 1900, the year he entered the real estate
business,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.420
blacks were relegated to a handful of overcrowded
neighborhoods,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.920
with substandard housing, and plagued by racial
violence.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.300
“The fight that I am making has got to be
made sooner or later," Payton wrote,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.640
"and I see no better time than now."

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.900
One of his first opportunities came in 1905
on Manhattan’s Upper West Side,

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.520
when according to one account two property
owners got into a dispute on an all-white block.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.280
One of them retaliated by inviting blacks
to move in to her two buildings.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.180
Whites fled, resulting in vacant apartments
on the block.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.960
Payton saw this as a business opportunity
to buy buildings at a discount

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.180
and sway more property owners to accept blacks
as tenants:

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.900
Why would landlords "keep their apartments
empty thereby losing much money in rent,"

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.300
he later wrote, "when there is an applicant
for it,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.440
and no other objection can be raised to him

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.780
other than that he has a black face?"

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.360
So he and a team of investors

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.340
started leasing and buying up properties on
West 99th street at a discount,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.740
one after another.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.500
Eventually, every building on the block was
renting to blacks.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.520
“The very prejudice which has heretofore
worked against us

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.649
can be turned and used to our profit,” Payton
wrote.

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:38.080
Located in a prime area of Manhattan that
bordered Central Park,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.260
West 99th and 98th Streets became home to
a black community that thrived until the mid-1950s,

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.720
when the government bulldozed these two blocks
to make way for a slum clearance project.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.340
A hundred and thirteen years after Payton
first de-segregated West 99th Street,

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.120
the former residents of this community still
hold annual reunions,

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:04.610
honoring the neighborhood where they were
born and raised.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.130
Payton's use of market forces to combat segregation

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.340
had an even bigger impact a couple miles uptown.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.360
Known as the father of Harlem, more than any
other figure, he was responsible for the formation

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.860
of what would become the cultural capital
of Black America.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.640
It all started on West 135th Street, when,
according to one account,

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.520
another dispute between two building owners
made it possible for blacks to start moving in.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.060
But in this case, bigoted white real estate
interests weren't going to give in without a fight.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.940
Enter the Hudson Realty Company,

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.080
with a board of directors that at one time
included

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.520
the co-founder of Bloomingdales department store,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.360
a former U.S. Ambassador, and his older brother.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.440
The company wanted to restore racial purity
on the block,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.060
and in 1904 it purchased four all-black buildings,
ordering everyone living there to get out.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.020
So Payton and his investors hit back.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.380
They got title to two buildings a few doors
down

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.460
and this time told the white people living there to move out.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.940
Then they invited the dispossessed black tenants
from up the block to take their place.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.980
Hudson Realty offered to buy Payton out, but
he refused,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.460
so the company gave up and pulled out altogether.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.700
Within a few years, black tenants inhabited
almost every building on West 135th.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.120
"Race prejudice is a luxury," Payton wrote,

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.120
"and like all luxuries can be made very expensive
in New York City."

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.640
But as Harlem's black community grew,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.720
white property owners tried to contain it
by forming real estate associations

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.480
governed by racial covenants that restricted
landlords from selling or renting to blacks.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.260
Again Payton struck back,

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.980
this time by reminding landlords that participating
in racial covenants

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.700
meant foregoing potential income,

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.060
because blacks typically paid more in rent.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.040
As he wrote in a 1908 ad,

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.160
“If that Colored Tenement of yours is not
paying you better than anything else you own,

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.720
SOMETHING IS WRONG."

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.640
With landlords choosing profit over prejudice,
over time, the racial covenants unraveled.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.060
In 1912, building owner Reginald Schenck told
the New York Times

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.440
that he rented his brownstone on 130th Street
to blacks

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.600
because "I can get more from negroes than from white
tenants."

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.160
The same year, Anna Lieb sold her building
on 136th

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.910
to a black family in violation of the racial
covenant

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.780
on the grounds that she had "a right to sell
to any person she saw fit."

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.410
In 1913, a widowed landlord on 137th was sued
by her next door neighbor

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.350
for welcoming blacks to her building.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:32.240
Before the judge could issue a injunction,
the neighbor dropped the case and joined her.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.120
A hundred years since Payton's death, there
are no monuments honoring him,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.700
or even a plaque on his former home.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.060
Yet black Harlem might not exist if it weren't
this visionary realtor

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.900
who saw that in a free market bigots can be
forced to pay a price for their prejudice

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.020
by making the color line costly.

